Ever since I played three hours of Silent Hill: Townfall and interviewed its writer and director Jon McKellan, one thing McKellan said has been rattling around in my head: "There are different Silent Hills for different people." It's a simple statement--one I agree with and felt silly for not having really considered until playing Silent Hill: Townfall.&nbsp; 



 Coming off the heels of Silent Hill f, the biggest departure in the 27-year-old series's formula both in location and gameplay, Townfall carries forward that same trajectory as its predecessor. Silent Hill seems to be entering something of an evolutionary moment, mutating and contorting as it separates itself more and more from the roots of its titular western town. Silent Hill: Townfall is different from other Silent Hills, both mechanically and thematically, in a way that shows this series still has far more to offer beyond its decrepit rusty aesthetics and Bubble Head Nurses. 



